WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.324
Rosa wants a health savings account.

00:00:02.324 --> 00:00:06.972
The law says she may have one only if she is covered

00:00:06.972 --> 00:00:11.232
by a high-deductible health plan and by no other disqualifying coverage.

00:00:11.232 --> 00:00:15.880
For 2026 an HDHP must have a deductible of at least 1,700

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:20.528
dollars for self-only coverage or 3,400 dollars for family coverage, and its

00:00:20.528 --> 00:00:25.175
out-of-pocket maximum may not exceed 8,500 dollars self-only or 17,000 dollars family.

00:00:25.175 --> 00:00:28.661
The plan may cover preventive care before the deductible.

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:32.534
These numbers are indexed each year, so learn the structure

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:36.020
and check the current figures before advising a client.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.487
To contribute to an HSA, Rosa must be covered by an HDHP

00:00:40.487 --> 00:00:44.954
on the first day of the month, must not have other first-dollar

00:00:44.954 --> 00:00:49.422
medical coverage such as a spouse’s traditional plan or a general purpose

00:00:49.422 --> 00:00:53.889
flexible spending account, must not be enrolled in Medicare, and must not

00:00:53.889 --> 00:00:57.612
be claimed as a dependent on someone else’s tax return.

00:00:57.612 --> 00:01:02.079
Permitted other coverage includes dental, vision, disability, long-term care and specific-disease policies.

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:05.429
Dennis, who enrolls in Medicare at 65, must stop

00:01:05.429 --> 00:01:08.780
contributing, although he may keep spending what he saved.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.130
The account belongs to the individual, not the employer.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:17.852
For 2026 the annual HSA contribution limit is 4,400 dollars for self-only

00:01:17.852 --> 00:01:23.575
coverage and 8,750 dollars for family coverage, from all sources combined, with

00:01:23.575 --> 00:01:28.821
an additional 1,000 dollars for account holders age 55 or older.

00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:34.066
Contributions are deductible, or excluded from income when made through payroll.

00:01:34.066 --> 00:01:35.497
Earnings grow tax-free.

00:01:35.497 --> 00:01:39.312
Withdrawals for qualified medical expenses, including deductibles, coinsurance,

00:01:39.312 --> 00:01:42.650
prescriptions, dental and vision care, are tax-free.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:47.896
Withdrawals for anything else are taxed as income plus a 20

00:01:47.896 --> 00:01:52.664
percent penalty, which disappears after age 65, death or disability.

00:01:52.664 --> 00:01:57.910
Unused balances roll over forever and move with the account holder.

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:00.608
An HRA is different in kind.

00:02:00.608 --> 00:02:04.654
Only the employer funds it; employees may not contribute.

00:02:04.654 --> 00:02:10.049
The employer owns the account and sets what it reimburses, typically deductibles,

00:02:10.049 --> 00:02:14.995
coinsurance and other medical expenses, and whether unused amounts carry over.

00:02:14.995 --> 00:02:17.692
Reimbursements are tax-free to the employee.

00:02:17.692 --> 00:02:21.739
An HRA need not be paired with an HDHP.

00:02:21.739 --> 00:02:27.583
Unlike an HSA, it usually does not travel with an employee who leaves.

00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:32.529
Newer variants let employers reimburse individual market premiums: the individual coverage

00:02:32.529 --> 00:02:37.474
HRA for any employer and the qualified small employer HRA for

00:02:37.474 --> 00:02:42.420
employers with fewer than 50 employees that offer no group plan.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.851
Put them side by side.

00:02:44.851 --> 00:02:50.200
The HSA is the individual’s account: funded by employee, employer or

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:55.548
both; requires an HDHP; capped by the annual limit; portable; invested;

00:02:55.548 --> 00:02:59.924
subject to the 20 percent penalty on nonmedical use.

00:02:59.924 --> 00:03:06.245
The HRA is the employer’s promise: funded only by the employer; pairs with

00:03:06.245 --> 00:03:12.566
any plan; limited only by the employer’s design, except the QSEHRA cap; stays

00:03:12.566 --> 00:03:17.914
with the employer; no investment account; no nonmedical withdrawals at all.

00:03:17.914 --> 00:03:24.235
Priya might offer an HDHP with HSA contributions to her bakery staff, or

00:03:24.235 --> 00:03:30.070
keep a traditional plan and add an HRA to soften its deductible.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:34.634
Rosa, self-employed and healthy, buys a 2026 HDHP with

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:38.691
a 3,000 dollar deductible and opens an HSA.

00:03:38.691 --> 00:03:45.791
She may contribute up to 4,400 dollars, deduct it, and pay her deductible from

00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:52.890
the account tax-free; what she does not spend keeps growing for retirement medical costs.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:58.975
Priya, employing twenty people, compares an HDHP with a 1,500 dollar employer

00:03:58.975 --> 00:04:04.554
HSA contribution against a traditional plan with a 2,000 dollar HRA.

00:04:04.554 --> 00:04:11.653
Both lower the employees’ exposure; only the HSA money belongs to the employees permanently.

00:04:11.653 --> 00:04:15.710
Lesson 57 adds flexible spending accounts and the

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:19.260
tax rules that tie these accounts together.
